N2  - ABSTRACT This review presents the state of the art in quantum key distribution (QKD) for satellite-to-ground free-space optical links. It first surveys continuous-variable, discrete-variable, entanglement-based, measurement-device-independent, and twin-field protocols, and discusses their feasibility under the losses and time-varying conditions of orbital passes. It also distinguishes protocol security from practical implementation security. The paper then reviews enabling technologies, including single-photon sources and detectors, followed by atmospheric impairments, time-evolving turbulence, aperture averaging, and adaptive optics. Finally, physical channel limits and the trust assumptions of satellite-network architectures are discussed. Overall, the literature indicates that discrete-variable decoy-state BB84 in LEO downlink configurations is the most mature near-term SatQKD option, including for daylight operation when appropriate filtering and pointing/turbulence mitigation are employed.
ER  - 

