N2  - Summary Deep space missions put electronic systems in harsh radiation conditions that can severely degrade the performance, cause faults, or cause outright system failure. The requirement of resilient, radiation-resistant electronics increases when space exploration missions come far enough to the point where spacecraft are no longer shielded by the magnetic field of the Earth. The chapter is a general overview of the mechanism with which radiation influences semiconductor devices, and they are total ionizing dose (TID), single-event effects (SEE), and displacement damage. It discusses the underlying design principles and the design strategies used to reduce these effects, both at the material level (innovation and design of circuits) and in the shielding of circuits and fault tolerance of the system as a whole. The focus is put on the traditional hardening methods and on the emerging technology, such as the use of commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) components in space projects. Using case studies and recent developments, the chapter describes the changing environment of space electronics, and provides some insight into future trends in building strong structures that can withstand the extreme radiations in deep space.
