N2  - Summary Mission system architecture is a comprehensive framework that defines how various components of a space mission interact to achieve its objectives. The Concept of Operations serves as the strategic framework that outlines the objectives, phases, and execution plans for a space mission, ensuring all components and activities are aligned to achieve mission goals. Space missions are structured into distinct phases to ensure the systematic execution of complex objectives. The phases provide a comprehensive framework for missions, including lunar exploration, interplanetary travel, and extended human habitation in extraterrestrial environments. Habitat features in space environments are designed to ensure the safety, comfort, and operational efficiency of astronauts during long-duration missions. Efficient waste collection and hygiene systems are essential for maintaining health and safety within space habitats. Ensuring astronaut well-being during space missions requires carefully designed systems for sleep accommodation, health monitoring, and clothing.
